1. What is File I/O in Java?
File I/O (Input/Output) is used to read data from and write data to files.

Java provides two major APIs:
java.io (Legacy / Stream-based)
java.nio / java.nio.file (Modern, faster, non-blocking support)

Interview one-liner
“Java File I/O allows persistent storage and retrieval of data using stream and NIO-based APIs.”

2. Types of I/O in Java
Based on Data Type

| Type             | Description | Examples          |
| ---------------- | ----------- | ----------------- |
| Byte Stream      | Binary data | `FileInputStream` |
| Character Stream | Text data   | `FileReader`      |

Based on Direction
| Type   | Examples                             |
| ------ | ------------------------------------ |
| Input  | `FileInputStream`, `BufferedReader`  |
| Output | `FileOutputStream`, `BufferedWriter` |

3. File Class (java.io.File) – Basics
Purpose

Represents file or directory metadata

❌ Does NOT read/write content

Example
import java.io.File;

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        File file = new File("test.txt");

        System.out.println(file.exists());
        System.out.println(file.getAbsolutePath());
    }
}

Output
false
C:\project\test.txt

4. Byte Streams (Binary Data)
4.1 FileOutputStream (Write)
import java.io.*;

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        FileOutputStream fos = new FileOutputStream("data.txt");
        fos.write("Java File IO".getBytes());
        fos.close();

        System.out.println("File written");
    }
}

Output
File written

4.2 FileInputStream (Read)
import java.io.*;

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream("data.txt");
        int ch;
        while ((ch = fis.read()) != -1) {
            System.out.print((char) ch);
        }
        fis.close();
    }
}

Output
Java File IO

5. Character Streams (Text Data – Preferred)
5.1 FileWriter (Write)
import java.io.*;

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        FileWriter fw = new FileWriter("text.txt");
        fw.write("Hello Java");
        fw.close();

        System.out.println("Done");
    }
}

Output
Done

5.2 FileReader (Read)
import java.io.*;

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        FileReader fr = new FileReader("text.txt");
        int ch;
        while ((ch = fr.read()) != -1) {
            System.out.print((char) ch);
        }
        fr.close();
    }
}

Output
Hello Java

6. Buffered Streams (🔥 VERY IMPORTANT)
Why Buffered?
Improves performance
Reduces disk I/O calls

BufferedWriter + BufferedReader
import java.io.*;

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {

        BufferedWriter bw = new BufferedWriter(new FileWriter("buffer.txt"));
        bw.write("Buffered IO Example");
        bw.newLine();
        bw.write("Java");
        bw.close();

        BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("buffer.txt"));
        String line;
        while ((line = br.readLine()) != null) {
            System.out.println(line);
        }
        br.close();
    }
}

Output
Buffered IO Example
Java

7. Try-With-Resources (Java 7+) 🔥
Problem (Before)

Must manually close streams

Solution
import java.io.*;

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        try (BufferedReader br =
                new BufferedReader(new FileReader("buffer.txt"))) {

            System.out.println(br.readLine());
        }
    }
}


✔ Auto-closes resources
✔ Cleaner & safer

8. Object Serialization (🔥 Interview Favorite)
8.1 Serializable Interface
import java.io.*;

class Employee implements Serializable {
    int id;
    String name;

    Employee(int id, String name) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
    }
}

8.2 Serialization (Write Object)
ObjectOutputStream oos =
    new ObjectOutputStream(new FileOutputStream("emp.ser"));
oos.writeObject(new Employee(1, "Sai"));
oos.close();

8.3 Deserialization (Read Object)
ObjectInputStream ois =
    new ObjectInputStream(new FileInputStream("emp.ser"));

Employee e = (Employee) ois.readObject();
System.out.println(e.id + " " + e.name);
ois.close();

Output
1 Sai

Key Points:
transient fields are NOT serialized
serialVersionUID recommended

9. Java NIO (Modern I/O) 🔥🔥
9.1 Path & Files API (Java 7+)
import java.nio.file.*;

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Path path = Paths.get("nio.txt");

        Files.write(path, "NIO File API".getBytes());

        String content = Files.readString(path);
        System.out.println(content);
    }
}

Output
NIO File API

Advantages of NIO:
Faster
Less boilerplate
Supports non-blocking I/O
Better for large files

